---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Re-permits the distinct fusion that `224`/`225` take away, on the one
# pipeline where taking it away buys nothing: an explicitly UNORDERED one
# (#975).
#
# `224`'s header spells out what it is protecting. The fused seen-set
# (`ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()`) makes `add()` thread-safe, but it does not
# fix WHICH element survives among equals: the JDK's `distinct()` is documented
# STABLE on an ordered stream, keeping the element first in ENCOUNTER order,
# while the set keeps whichever equal element wins the race to `add()`. For
# equal-but-distinguishable objects that is observable and non-deterministic,
# so on a parallel pipeline the distinct has to stay native.
#
# That contract is conditional on the stream being ordered, and
# `BaseStream.unordered()` removes exactly that condition: once a stream is
# unordered, `distinct()` is specified to be free to keep ANY element among
# equals, which is precisely what the concurrent seen-set already delivers. So
# there is nothing left to protect, and this is the case where the fusion is
# worth most, since a parallel pipeline is the one running over a large input.
#
# ONLY the call BEFORE the distinct counts, which is why this rule has no `225`
# twin. `unordered()` marks the stream unordered from that point on, so
# `src.unordered().parallel().distinct()` hands `distinct` an unordered stream
# and the stability contract never applies; `src.distinct().parallel()
# .unordered()` runs the distinct on a stream that is still ordered, and
# relaxing it there would break the very contract `224` exists to keep.
#
# HOW, rather than a `when` clause on `224`: phino patterns cannot test a
# binding group for the ABSENCE of a call, which is why `224` takes the
# positive route in the first place, and a rule that stripped a marker back off
# would only hand `224` another chance — the whole streams/* selection runs to
# fixpoint inside one phino invocation, so a later rule's output is re-offered
# to an earlier one and the pair would oscillate (#801). Instead the mark is
# MONOTONE: this rule adds `unordered ↦ Φ.true` to the pragma and nothing ever
# removes it. `224` and `225` are closed on the three-binding form jeo's `216`
# emits, so a marked pragma simply stops matching them, and this rule is closed
# on the UNmarked form, so it cannot re-fire on its own output. `307` carries a
# trailing wildcard for the marker and folds both forms into the same distill.
#
# Detection is method-local, exactly as `224`'s is, and that locality cuts the
# other way here: `224` over-reverts, which only forfeits an optimisation,
# whereas this rule over-permits. A method holding an unrelated
# `Stream.unordered()` chain alongside a parallel ORDERED distinct over
# equal-but-distinguishable objects would keep a fusion it should not have. The
# guard on `𝑒-uclass` narrows that — the `unordered()` must be on the same
# stream interface the distinct is on, so an `IntStream.unordered()` elsewhere
# in the method does not count — but the residue is real, and widening from a
# local match to a dataflow one is the follow-up that would remove it.
#
# `skip()` and `dropWhile()` have unordered contracts of their own (any n
# elements, any subset of the matching prefix), so the same argument extends to
# `222`/`223` and `226`/`227` — but only once their counter and latch are
# atomic, which they are not: they are a plain `long[1]` and a plain `int[1]`,
# raced rather than merely unstable. This rule stays with distinct for that
# reason.
name: unordered-permits-distinct
pattern: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-unordered ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.𝜏-uop,
      𝜏-uclass ↦ 𝑒-uclass,
      𝜏-umethod ↦ 𝑒-umethod,
      𝐵-unordered-rest
    ⟧,
    𝐵-between,
    𝜏-distinct ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.hone.distinct,
      class ↦ 𝑒-class,
      stream-class ↦ 𝑒-stream-class
    ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-unordered ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.𝜏-uop,
      𝜏-uclass ↦ 𝑒-uclass,
      𝜏-umethod ↦ 𝑒-umethod,
      𝐵-unordered-rest
    ⟧,
    𝐵-between,
    𝜏-distinct ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.hone.distinct,
      class ↦ 𝑒-class,
      stream-class ↦ 𝑒-stream-class,
      unordered ↦ Φ.true
    ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
when:
  and:
    - or:
        - eq: [𝜏-uop, 'invokeinterface']
        - eq: [𝜏-uop, 'invokevirtual']
    - eq: [𝑒-umethod, '"unordered"']
    - eq: [𝑒-uclass, 𝑒-stream-class]
